                <pb facs="0538" n="424" sameAs="frus:frus1931v01/pg424"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15a3/1625: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Marriner</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-08-22T13:00:00+01:00">August 22, 1931—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 22—9 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>525. Massigli this morning told me that he was sending by the diplomatic pouch
                    tonight to Rome the latest French proposal regarding the naval difficulties. It
                    was not possible to communicate the details, he said, until Italy had studied
                    them, but he assured me that he would pass them on to me as soon as he had
                    received word from Rome that the proposal had been received and was being
                    studied, presumably by next Thursday. At the same time, the details would be
                    sent to the British Government. Massigli added that in his opinion the proposal
                    would form an adequate basis for solution of the controversy. It had received
                    the approval of all the necessary authorities in France.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Rome and London.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Marriner</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
